A presidential election was held in [[Portugal]] on 24 January 2021.<ref>{{cite web|title=O Presidente da República, Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa, marcou hoje as eleições presidenciais para 24 de janeiro de 2021.|url=https://24.sapo.pt/atualidade/artigos/presidente-da-republica-marca-eleicoes-presidenciais-para-24-de-janeiro|website=www.24.sapo.pt|publisher=Sapo|access-date=24 November 2020|language=pt}}</ref> The incumbent [[President of Portugal|President]], [[Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa]], was reelected for a second term.

The elections were held during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Portugal|COVID-19 pandemic]], and Portugal was under a lockdown as of election day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-portugal-election-idUSKBN29S0BU|title=Portugal holds presidential election as COVID-19 cases spiral|publisher=Reuters|date=23 January 2021}}</ref> President [[Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa]] was reelected by a [[Landslide victory|landslide]], winning 60.7 percent of the votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.eu/article/president-rebelo-de-sousa-wins-landslide-re-election-in-portugal/|title=President Rebelo de Sousa wins landslide reelection in Portugal|publisher=Politico|date=24 January 2021}}</ref> He won every district in the country and all 308 municipalities, a result which happened for the first time ever in Portuguese democracy; he won 3,083 parishes out of 3,092.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://observador.pt/2021/01/25/as-nove-freguesias-em-3-092/|title=As nove freguesias — em 3.092 — onde Marcelo não ganhou|publisher=Observador|date=25 January 2021}}</ref> The election also marked the rise of [[right-wing]] candidate [[André Ventura]], leader of [[Chega]], who polled 3rd with almost 12 percent of the votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sicnoticias.pt/especiais/eleicoes-presidenciais/2021-01-25-Marcelo-dominou-Ana-Gomes-foi-2.-mas-Ventura-venceu-mais-distritos.-Esquerda-caiu-a-pique|title=Marcelo dominou, Ana Gomes foi 2.ª mas Ventura venceu mais distritos. Esquerda caiu a pique|publisher=SIC Notícias|date=25 January 2021|access-date=2021-01-25|archive-date=2021-03-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210301005809/https://sicnoticias.pt/especiais/eleicoes-presidenciais/2021-01-25-Marcelo-dominou-Ana-Gomes-foi-2.-mas-Ventura-venceu-mais-distritos.-Esquerda-caiu-a-pique|url-status=dead}}</ref> In second place, former MEP and Ambassador [[Ana Gomes]] was able to win 13 percent of the votes, the best result ever for a female candidate in a presidential election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.publico.pt/2021/01/24/politica/noticia/presidenciais-ana-gomes-mulher-votada-1947739|title=Presidenciais: Ana Gomes é a mulher mais votada de sempre |publisher=Público|date=24 January 2021}}</ref> As in the [[2016 Portuguese presidential election|2016 elections]], the [[Socialist Party (Portugal)|Socialist Party]] (PS) did not officially endorse any candidate, despite Ana Gomes being a PS member.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://expresso.pt/politica/2020-09-08-PS-dividido-entre-os-apoios-declarados-a-Marcelo-e-o-silencio-dos-apoiantes-de-Ana-Gomes|title=PS dividido entre os apoios declarados a Marcelo e o silêncio dos apoiantes de Ana Gomes |publisher=Expresso|access-date=19 January 2025|date=8 September 2020}}</ref> The rest of candidates did not receive above 5 percent each.

Voter turnout fell to 39 percent, a drop of nine [[percentage point]]s, mainly due to the automatic registration of overseas voters; this practice increased the number of registered voters to almost 11 million.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.tsf.pt/portugal/politica/recenseamento-automatico-de-emigrantes-fez-subir-abstencao-seis-pontos-13273900.html|title=Recenseamento automático de emigrantes fez subir abstenção seis pontos|publisher=TSF Radio|date=25 January 2021}}</ref> In Portugal alone, turnout stood at 45.45 percent, a decrease of 4.6 percentage points compared to 2016. This was the lowest drop in turnout in an election with an incumbent running since [[1980 Portuguese presidential election|1980]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.publico.pt/2021/01/26/politica/noticia/eleitores-estrangeiros-abstencao-5446-1947997|title=Sem eleitores no estrangeiros, abstenção foi de 54,46% |publisher=Público|date=26 January 2021}}</ref> Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa was sworn in for a second term as President on 9 March 2021.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/comeca-segundo-mandato-marcelo-toma-posse-como-presidente-da-republica_n1303020 |title=Começa segundo mandato. Marcelo toma posse como Presidente da República  |language=pt |work=RTP |access-date=18 February 2026 |date=9 March 2021}}</ref>

==Background==
Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa was elected president with 52% of the votes in January 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.publico.pt/2016/01/24/politica/noticia/marcelo-rebelo-de-sousa-eleito-presidente-1721277|title=Marcelo ganha à primeira com dobro dos votos de Nóvoa |publisher=Público|date=24 January 2016}}</ref> In Portugal, the president is the [[head of state]] and has mostly ceremonial powers.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://regiao-sul.pt/2021/01/24/internacional/presidente-de-portugal-rebelo-de-sousa-ganha-novo-mandato-em-cedula-socialmente-distanciada/527324|title=Presidente de Portugal, Rebelo de Sousa, ganha novo mandato em cédula socialmente distanciada |publisher=Diário Regiao Sul|date=24 January 2021}}</ref> However, the president does have some political influence, and can [[Dissolution of Parliament|dissolve]] [[Assembly of the Republic (Portugal)|Parliament]] if a crisis occurs. The president's [[official residence]] is the [[Belém Palace]] in Lisbon.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|url=https://www.presidencia.pt/?idc=1|title=Funções do Presidente |publisher=Presidency of the Republic}}</ref> Throughout his first term, Marcelo had to cohabitate with Socialist prime minister António Costa,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://rr.sapo.pt/2016/03/09/politica/a-tomada-de-posse-de-marcelo-em-imagens/noticia/48872/|title=A tomada de posse de Marcelo em imagens|publisher=Rádio Renascença|date=9 March 2016|access-date=25 January 2021|archive-date=9 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309103657/https://rr.sapo.pt/2016/03/09/politica/a-tomada-de-posse-de-marcelo-em-imagens/noticia/48872/|url-status=dead}}</ref> with the institutional relationship described as very good.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2024/12/04/politica/noticia/marcelo-recorda-coabitacao-costa-eramos-felizes-nao-sabiamos-2114494 |title=Marcelo recorda coabitação com Costa: “Éramos felizes e não sabíamos”  |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=4 December 2024}}</ref> During his term as head of state, Marcelo also had [[Opinion polling for the 2021 Portuguese presidential election#Approval ratings|high approval ratings]], influenced by his "affections presidency" style and his empathetic response to national disasters, such as the [[June 2017 Portugal wildfires|2017 wildfires]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2018/01/21/politica/noticia/sim-o-afecto-na-politica-faz-crescer-o-sucesso-de-um-pais-1799974 |title=Sim, o afecto na política conta para o sucesso de um país |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=26 August 2026| date=21 January 2018}}</ref> The last year of Marcelo's first term was dominated by the Covid-19 pandemic, with the head of state declaring a [[state of emergency]] at the government's request.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jpn.up.pt/2020/03/18/presidente-da-republica-decreta-estado-de-emergencia/ |title=Presidente da República decreta estado de emergência |language=pt |work=JPN-JornalismoPortoNet |access-date=27 August 2026| date=18 March 2020}}</ref>

===Pre-campaign===
Early on, the Social Democrats (PSD) declared their support for Marcelo's re-election,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://observador.pt/2020/09/26/comissao-politica-do-psd-declara-apoio-a-marcelo-rebelo-de-sousa-nas-presidenciais/ |title=PSD declara formalmente apoio a Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa nas presidenciais: "É o que dá mais garantias de equilíbrio" |language=pt |work=Observador |access-date=27 August 2026| date=26 September 2020}}</ref> while the president only announced his re-election bid in December 2020, just over a month before election day.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2020/12/07/politica/noticia/marcelo-anunciar-hoje-recandidatura-1942024 |title=Marcelo anuncia recandidatura numa pastelaria em Belém  |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=7 December 2020}}</ref> On the Socialist Party (PS), prime minister António Costa also expressed, from early on, his "endorsement" to a second term for Marcelo,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2020/05/13/politica/noticia/costa-lanca-marcelo-segundo-mandato-belem-1916378 |title=Costa lança Marcelo para um segundo mandato em Belém. Marcelo tira tapete a Centeno  |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=13 May 2020}}</ref> but the prime minister's position created divisions on the left and within his own party.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jn.pt/nacional/artigo/apoio-de-costa-a-marcelo-gera-criticas-a-esquerda/12200487 |title=Apoio de Costa a Marcelo gera críticas à esquerda |language=pt |work=Jornal de Notícias |access-date=27 August 2026| date=15 May 2020}}</ref><ref name="AG2021">{{cite news |url=https://expresso.pt/politica/2020-05-17-Deprimente.-Grave.-Perigoso.-Ana-Gomes-arrasa-apoio-de-Costa-a-Marcelo-e-anuncia-reflexao-sobre-presidenciais |title=“Deprimente”. “Grave”. “Perigoso”. Ana Gomes arrasa apoio de Costa a Marcelo e anuncia “reflexão” sobre presidenciais |language=pt |work=Expresso |access-date=27 August 2026| date=17 May 2020}}</ref> After this, former PS MEP [[Ana Gomes]], who strongly criticized Costa's stance,<ref name="AG2021"/> announced she was considering a run, which she confirmed in September 2020.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2020/09/07/politica/noticia/ana-gomes-candidata-presidencia-republica-1930706 |title=Ana Gomes é candidata à Presidência da República  |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=7 September 2020}}</ref> Sole [[Chega]] party MP, [[André Ventura]], also announced his candidacy just a few months after being elected to parliament in the [[2019 Portuguese legislative election|2019 legislative election]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2020/02/08/politica/noticia/andre-ventura-anuncia-candidatura-presidente-republica-1903369 |title=André Ventura anuncia candidatura a Presidente da República  |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=8 February 2020}}</ref> while the [[Left Bloc]] repeated the candidacy of [[Marisa Matias]],<ref name="MM21"/> the communists presented MEP [[João Ferreira (politician)|João Ferreira]]<ref name="JF21"/> and the [[Liberal Initiative]], also a new party that gained representation in the 2019 legislative election, declared [[Tiago Mayan Gonçalves]] as their presidential candidate.<ref name="TMF21"/>

==Electoral system==
Under [[Law of Portugal|Portuguese law]], a candidate must receive a majority of votes (50% plus one vote) to be elected. If no candidate achieved a majority in the first round, a [[runoff election]] (i.e., second round, held between the two candidates who received the most votes in the first round) should be held.<ref>{{cite web|title=LEI ELEITORAL DO PRESIDENTE DA REPÚBLICA|url=https://www.portaldoeleitor.pt/Documents/Doc_Lei_PR.pdf|publisher=Portal do Eleitor}}</ref>

In order to stand for election, candidates must be of Portuguese origin and over 35 years old, gather 7,500 signatures of support one month before the election and submit them to the [[Constitutional Court of Portugal]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/lexionario/termo/presidente-republica|title=Presidente da República |publisher=[[Diário da República]] |access-date=19 January 2025}}</ref> The Constitutional Court then certifies the candidacies which meet the requirements to appear on the ballot. The highest number of candidacies ever accepted was ten in 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://dre.pt/web/guest/legislacao-consolidada/-/lc/148081979/202101110418/73914847/diploma/indice?consolidacaoTag=Elei%C3%A7%C3%B5es|title=Regulamenta a eleição do Presidente da República|publisher=Diário da República}}</ref>

===Early voting===
For the first time in a Presidential election, voters were also able to [[Early voting|vote early]], which would happen one week before election day on 17 January 2021. Voters had to register between 10 and 14 January in order to be eligible to cast an early ballot; a total of 246,880 voters requested to vote early in 2021.<ref>[https://eco.sapo.pt/2021/01/16/portugueses-comecam-a-votar-nas-presidenciais-este-domingo-e-ha-246-mil-inscritos/ "Portugueses começam a votar nas Presidenciais este domingo e há 246 mil inscritos "], ''Eco'', 16 January 2021. Retrieved 17 January 2021.</ref> On 17 January, 197,903 voters (80.16 percent of voters that registered) cast an early [[ballot]].<ref name=":0" /><ref>[https://eco.sapo.pt/2021/01/21/80-dos-inscritos-votaram-no-domingo-para-as-presidenciais/ "80% dos inscritos votaram no domingo para as presidenciais"], ''Eco'', 21 January 2021. Retrieved 21 January 2021.</ref>

==Candidates==
[[File:Eleições presidenciais 2021.png|thumb|right|Official logo of the election.]]
There were seven candidates certified to run in this election. In addition, the [[Constitutional Court (Portugal)|Constitutional Court]] rejected Eduardo Baptista's nomination due to insufficient signatures, although his name still appeared on the ballot, albeit votes cast for him would be counted as invalid. Six more individuals had announced their intention to run for President, but did not present any application to the Court, two of whom publicly stated that they would withdraw, which includes [[Democratic Republican Party (Portugal)|Democratic Republican Party]] (PDR) leader [[Bruno Fialho]] who, despite saying he had enough signatures, decided to drop out in order "not to spend thousands of euros on the country".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lusa |date=2020-12-12 |title=Bruno Fialho, do PDR, desiste de candidatura para “não gastar milhares de euros ao país” |url=https://www.publico.pt/2020/12/12/politica/noticia/bruno-fialho-pdr-desiste-candidatura-nao-gastar-milhares-euros-pais-1942738 |access-date=2025-01-06 |website=Público |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Finally, three more individuals were, for a while, thought of as potential or speculative candidates, but later ruled out any type of candidacy.

